 ## Background
-The shift from implicit to explicit representations in radiance fields emerged to address the computational bottlenecks of ray-marching and the spectral bias of MLPs. Early works like Point-NeRF [15538-point-nerf-representation] introduced neural point clouds to replace global MLPs, while Gaussian Splatting variants [14273-viewing-direction-gaussian-splatting] leveraged differentiable rasterization for real-time performance. Differentiable Primitive Assembly [13657-differentiable-primitive-assembly] further explored explicit geometry via quadrics. Frequency-modulated approaches [15903-frequency-modulated-point-cloud-rendering] addressed high-frequency detail loss. GARF [S1] explored Gaussian activations for pose estimation. These methods are widely used in real-time rendering, SLAM, and editable 3D scene reconstruction.
+The shift from implicit to explicit representations in radiance fields emerged to address the computational bottlenecks of volume rendering and the lack of explicit geometric control in standard NeRF. While early NeRF variants relied on dense MLPs, methods like Gaussian Splatting and Point-NeRF introduced explicit primitives to enable real-time rendering. Differentiable Primitive Assembly (DPA) further explored explicit shape abstraction using quadrics. GARF (April 2022) introduced Gaussian activations as an alternative to positional encoding, specifically targeting pose estimation from unknown camera poses [S1]. These methods collectively form a family of "explicit" or "primitive-based" radiance fields that prioritize geometric interpretability and rendering efficiency over the purely implicit continuity of standard NeRF.
 
